38,Smart Pricing in Online Marketplaces: A Machine Learning and Analytics Framework,"Faysal Ahmed, Humayra Akter, Afranan Bintha Rahman, Arindam Chatterjee, T. M. Shahriar Sazzad",2025,"Abstract

Accurately predicting prices of electronic devices in online marketplaces is essential for keeping prices fair and competitive. For sellers, it supports to set prices that are attractive to buyers after keeping profit. For buyers, it helps to ensure that they pay a fair amount for the actual value of the product. As e-commerce is growing continuously, such data-driven pricing has become important for decision-making on both sides of the market. This research focuses on laptops and represents a machine learning-based approach to estimate prices using hardware specifications. In total, fourteen regression models are evaluated. The proposed stacked ensemble model which is combining Linear Regression, Ridge Regression, RANSAC Regression, Decision Tree and Histogram-Based Gradient Boosting achieved the best performance with the value of $\mathbf{R}^{\mathbf{2}} \mathbf{8 6 3 3}$. To make it practical, the final model is deployed as a Streamlit web application, which allows users to enter laptop specifications and give real-time price predictions. Although this work focuses on laptops, the same approach can be extended to other electronic devices. Therefore, it is a flexible and scalable pricing tool for e-commerce platforms.",https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/abstract/document/11367595/
39,Estimating solubility of nitrogen in various ionic liquids: application of machine-learning,"Kassem Al Attabi, Farzona Alimova, Anupam Yadav, H. S. Shreenidhi, Abhinav Kumar, Devendra Singh, Vatsal Jain, Zainab Ahmed Hamodi, Aseel Smerat, Ahmad Khalid",16 February 2026,"Abstract
The limited solubility of nitrogen (N2) in conventional solvents such as water severely restricts the efficiency of ammonia synthesis, motivating the search for alternative media. Ionic liquids (ILs), with their tunable physicochemical properties, offer a promising solution, yet the vast chemical diversity of cations and anions makes experimental screening highly complex. This study aims to develop robust machine-learning models capable of accurately predicting N2 solubility in diverse ILs using COSMO-based descriptors. A comprehensive set of algorithms, including Artificial Neural Networks (ANNs), Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs), Random Forests, CatBoost, Support Vector Regression (SVR), and others underwent training and validation with a dataset comprising 385 points in total, with performance assessed via numerous metrics and visual analyses. The results demonstrate that ANN, CNN, Random Forest, CatBoost, and SVR achieved superior predictive performance, with SHAP analysis identifying pressure and specific descriptors (e.g., S5) as dominant factors influencing solubility. The novelty of this work lies in integrating advanced ML models with interpretable SHAP-based analysis, providing both predictive accuracy and mechanistic insights into N2–IL interactions. While challenges remain due to the limited size of available datasets and the immense diversity of IL structures, this study highlights the potential of data-driven approaches to accelerate IL design. Future perspectives include expanding experimental databases and incorporating hybrid physics-informed ML frameworks to further enhance generalizability and guide rational IL development for energy and environmental applications.",https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11696-025-04623-2
