This work aims to develop a predictive model that accurately forecasts Air Quality Index (AQI) levels in Delhi, one of the world's most polluted cities. For this, we explore the application of Machine Learning (ML) models including Linear Regression, Lasso Regression, Ridge Regression, Decision Tree, Random Forest, AdaBoost, CatBoost, LightGBM and XGBoost. The study uses a comprehensive dataset of daily air quality measurement which contains various pollutants. All models are evaluated using 5-fold cross-validation and a hold-out method. Experimental results reveal that different models excel under the two validation techniques. In the 5-fold cross-validation experiments, the CatBoost model outperforms the other models, achieving an R<sup xmlns:mml=""http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"" xmlns:xlink=""http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink"">2</sup> of 0.9153, MSE of 822.80, MAE of 20.29, RMSE of 28.62, and MAPE of 11.44. For the hold-out validation, the AdaBoost model with parameters optimized through a Randomized Search CV delivers the best performance, yielding a superior R<sup xmlns:mml=""http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"" xmlns:xlink=""http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink"">2</sup> of 0.9208, MAE of 19.00, and RMSE of 27.13. These results collectively favour the use of advanced ML models to accurately analyze and predict AQI.",https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/abstract/document/11451451/
