279,FPGA Implementation of Regression Algorithms for Real-Time Applications: A Comprehensive Survey,"Velayutham Sudharsan, Shirshendu Roy",2026,"Abstract

Real-time inference at the edge demands bounded worst-case latency, sustained throughput and a tight power envelope under single-sample workloads, constraints CPUs and GPUs cannot satisfy simultaneously. Field-Programmable Gate Arrays (FPGAs) close this gap: their spatial execution model computes every data path operator in parallel, timing is deterministic by construction, and for single-sample inference the energy advantage over GPUs reaches three to four orders of magnitude. Although FPGA-based regression now spans biomedical engineering, industrial automation, wireless communications, automotive systems, scientific instrumentation, smart grids and Internet-of-Things (IoT) edge nodes, the literature remains fragmented and no prior review treats the full regression spectrum under a single analytical frame. This survey provides six contributions: a two-dimensional taxonomy classifying implementations by algorithmic family and implementation dimension; a hardware-aware analysis of twenty-one regression variants; a systematic mapping across nine real-time application domains; standardized latency-accuracy and energy-accuracy Pareto comparisons; a focused 2022-2025 review covering hls4ml, FINN, AI-engine integration, online learning and posit arithmetic; and fifteen prioritized open re-search challenges. Consistent findings emerge: linear and regularized methods dominate the latency frontier below 64 input dimensions, Extreme Learning Machines occupy an overlooked efficiency sweet spot, hls4ml-based deployment has reached practical maturity, and standardized benchmarking remains the most pressing infrastructure gap.",https://journal.uob.edu.bh/items/0f8410c8-7211-4759-a609-6ddff30b4cb5
